message.content:
{
  "summary": "You present a clear Great Barrier Reef monitoring concept, explain why one spacecraft is proposed, and use STK visualisations to cover key mission segments. The main limitation is that errors in the orbit design undermine the mission analysis, while the operator, customer organisations, and ground-station capability need clearer definition. Correcting these points and strengthening the supporting evidence will make your conclusions more dependable.",
  "next_steps": [
    "Recheck the orbit calculations and update the STK model and figures: at 600 km, the sun-synchronous inclination is approximately 97.8° retrograde, and the semi-major axis is 6971 km using the stated Earth radius. Define RAAN in relation to launch timing and epoch, and provide the STK project files so your results can be checked.",
    "Verify the launch and communications assumptions. Use Kourou’s approximate latitude of 5.2° N, and substantiate Mount Pleasant’s suitability with TT&C antenna, frequency, and link-performance evidence. Reconcile the 580.786-second access duration with your text, and specify local solar time and eclipse conditions.",
    "Name the responsible spacecraft operator and specific customer organisations. Strengthen evidence for imaging and communications performance, then use that evidence in your conclusion. Review figure and table numbering, consistency of prose and formatting, and reference completeness; replace Wikipedia with an appropriate source."
  ],
  "criteria": {
    "C1": "Your Introduction clearly explains the reef-monitoring purpose and environmental rationale, and justifies using one spacecraft for simplicity and cost. You identify marine and conservation scientists as users. To reach the next rubric level, name the responsible operator and specific customer organisations, and support the discussion with appropriate references.",
    "C2": "Your Launch and mission-segment sections cover the requested topics and include useful STK visualisations. However, the stated 87.79° inclination, 7171 km semi-major axis, and Kourou latitude of 45.05° are incorrect; the ground-station capability is also unsubstantiated. The next rubric level requires corrected, verifiable design results and stronger evidence, including STK files.",
    "C3": "Your Conclusion recaps the launch, orbit, and proposed ground facility, and notes a launch-vehicle risk. Its conclusions about orbit and illumination are not adequately supported by the design results. To reach the next rubric level, draw specific conclusions from corrected calculations and evidence for mission, imaging, and communications performance.",
    "C4": "Your report has a logical structure, contents list, executive summary, headings, figures, and bibliography. To reach the next rubric level, make prose and formatting consistent, number figures and tables clearly, complete and check references, and use sources appropriate to the task."
  }
}
